X2={'glance':'The names show how one day is shared across regions: Mukkoṭi in Telugu and Kannada homes, Svargavāsal in Tamil Nadu and Kerala, Mokṣadā in the Hindi belt and in ISKCON calendars. What unites them is the idea of liberation, mokṣa, made present for a day.',
 'ekadashi':'Ekādaśī is also kept by Śaiva and Smārta families, and by many who keep no other fast. Its popularity owes much to the Vaiṣṇava saints: the Haridāsas of Karnataka, the Vārkarīs of Maharashtra walking to Paṇḍharpur on Āṣāḍhī Ekādaśī, and the Gauḍīya tradition, which made strict Ekādaśī a mark of devotion.',
 'margashirsha':'In Karnataka and Andhra, Dhanurmāsa is also a month when weddings and house-warmings are traditionally avoided, since the month is given to God. Rangoli at dawn, temple bells before sunrise and the smell of hot pongal define it in many towns.',
 'gate':'In Śrīvaiṣṇava thought, the gate also recalls a famous verse of Nammāḻvār describing the welcome the soul receives in Vaikuṇṭha: the gods line the way and the doors are thrown open. The temple rite stages that welcome for the living.',
 'story':'Other tellings link the day to the end of Viṣṇu’s cosmic sleep or to the gods’ dawn. Whatever the origin story, the practice is ancient in Tamil temples; the opening of the Paramapada Vāsal at Srirangam is described in temple chronicles centuries old.',
 'srirangam':'The Pagal Pattu days cover the first thousand verses (Periyāḻvār, Āṇḍāḷ, Kulaśekhara and others) and the Rāppattu nights the Tiruvāymoḻi. Each day Namperumāḷ is carried in a different splendid adornment, and pilgrims from across the South plan the year around it.',
 'tirumala':'Tirumala’s Vaikuṇṭha Ekādaśī crowds are among the largest single-day gatherings at any temple. Advance tokens, special queue lines and the ten-day opening were introduced to manage them; the details change from year to year, so pilgrims check TTD’s announcements.',
 'karnataka':'Karnataka’s Haridāsa poets, Purandaradāsa and Kanakadāsa among them, sang many songs on Ekādaśī and on fasting with devotion rather than for show. Their kīrtanas are still sung in night vigils across the state.',
 'mokshada':'Every Ekādaśī of the year has such a māhātmya, a glorification story, often set as a dialogue between Kṛṣṇa and Yudhiṣṭhira. Together they form a popular literature that families read on each Ekādaśī.',
 'gitajayanti':'For first-time readers, teachers often suggest beginning with Chapter 2 (the soul and duty), Chapter 12 (devotion, only twenty verses) or Chapter 15 (the inverted tree, also twenty verses). Reading one chapter slowly with its meaning is better than rushing through all eighteen.',
 'fast':'Fasting in Indian tradition is also a discipline of the senses and tongue: less food, less talk, more prayer. Many keep a vow of silence for part of the day, or avoid screens and entertainment, giving the time to reading and chanting.',
 'vigil':'In Andhra and Karnataka homes, night vigils are filled with Haridāsa songs and Annamācārya kīrtanas; in the North, with kīrtan and Hanumān Cālīsā; in Tamil Nadu, with the Divya Prabandham. The vigil looks different everywhere, but its purpose is the same: to welcome the dawn awake.',
 'parana':'If the pāraṇa window is very early or very short, families often wake before dawn, offer prayers and break the fast with a sip of water and a tulasī leaf, then eat properly later in the morning.',
 'andal':'Āṇḍāḷ’s story is loved: she wore the garlands meant for the god before they were offered, and Viṣṇu declared he preferred them so. She is said to have merged with Raṅganātha at Srirangam, linking her month of songs to the temple of the gate.'}
